      ],
      "certainty": "Possible",
      "description": "Information on update: Warning area removed from Northern Ireland and central Scotland as winds have eased here. End time of warning brought forward to 6pm on Tuesday. Strong west to southwesterly winds are expected to continue across northern Scotland during Tuesday. Gusts of 45-55 mph are possible quite widely but some coastal or exposed areas in the far north of Scotland could see gusts of 70 mph. The strongest of the winds will reduce from the southwest later on Tuesday, with the far north of Scotland the last to see winds ease.\n\n\n\nFor further details see https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/weather/warnings-and-advice/uk-warnings",
      "event": "Yellow wind warning",
      "expires": "2026-10-27T18:00:00+00:00",
      "headline": "A spell of strong winds may lead to damaging gusts and transport disruption.: UPDATED",
      "instruction": "If you are on the coast, stay safe during stormy weather by being aware of large waves. Even from the shore large breaking waves can sweep you off your feet and out to sea. Take care if walking near cliffs; know your route and keep dogs on a lead. In an emergency, call 999 and ask for the Coastguard.\n\nPrepare to protect your property and people from injury. Check for loose items outside your home and plan how you could secure them. Items include: bins, garden furniture, trampolines, tents, sheds, and fences.\n\nPeople cope better with power cuts when they have prepared for them in advance. It's easy to do; consider gathering torches and batteries, a mobile phone power pack and other essential items.\n\nGive yourself the best chance of avoiding delays by checking road conditions if driving, or bus and train timetables, amending your travel plans if necessary.\n\nBe prepared for weather warnings to change quickly. When a weather warning is issued, the Met Office recommends staying up to date with the weather forecast in your area.",
      "language": "en-GB",
      "onset": "2026-10-27T03:00:00+00:00",
